<date>2026-11-06</date>
<releaseinfo>1.0.0</releaseinfo>

<abstract>
<para>
&tdesystemd; brings &systemd; administration to the Trinity Desktop
Environment. It consists of two applications, the &manager; for services,
timers, date and time, hostname and locale, and the &journalviewer; for
reading and maintaining the system journal. Both are also available as
modules of the &tde; Control Center.
</para>
</abstract>

<keywordset>
<keyword>TDE</keyword>
<keyword>systemd</keyword>
<keyword>systemctl</keyword>
<keyword>journal</keyword>
<keyword>journalctl</keyword>
<keyword>services</keyword>
<keyword>polkit</keyword>
<keyword>kcontrol</keyword>
</keywordset>

</bookinfo>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<chapter id="introduction">
<title>Introduction</title>

<para>
&systemd; is the system and service manager used by most Linux
distributions. It starts and supervises services (daemons), runs timers,
keeps a structured log called the <emphasis>journal</emphasis> and offers
small services to configure the hostname, the system clock and the system
locale. &tdesystemd; is a graphical front end to all of this, designed to
look and behave like any other &tde; application.
</para>

<para>&tdesystemd; contains:</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>
<command>tde-systemd-manager</command>, the &manager;: shows the state of
the system, lists services and other units of the system and of your user
session, lets you start, stop, enable or mask them, shows timers, and
changes the date and time, the hostname and the system locale.
See <xref linkend="manager"/>.
</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>
<command>tde-journal-viewer</command>, the &journalviewer;: reads the
system or user journal, filters it by boot, priority, unit, identifier,
time and text, follows new messages as they arrive, exports them and
performs journal maintenance. See <xref linkend="journal"/>.
</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>
Two &tde; Control Center modules, <guilabel>Systemd Services</guilabel>
and <guilabel>System Journal</guilabel>, that embed the same views. See
<xref linkend="kcontrol-modules"/>.
</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>
<command>tde-systemd-helper</command>, a small privileged helper that
performs changes to the system after you have been authorized through
&polkit;. See <xref linkend="authorization"/>.
</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>
Everything that only reads information works with your normal user
rights. Changes to the system ask for the administrator password
(or your own password, depending on the &polkit; configuration of your
distribution). Changes to your own user services never need any extra
rights.
</para>

<sect1 id="requirements">
<title>Requirements</title>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>A system booted with &systemd; (version 245 or newer is recommended).</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>The &tde; R14.1 libraries.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><command>pkexec</command> from &polkit; and a running &polkit;
authentication agent such as <application>polkit-agent-tde</application>.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>For the optional pages: <command>systemd-timedated</command>
(date and time), <command>systemd-hostnamed</command> (hostname) and
<command>systemd-localed</command> (locale and keyboard). Pages whose
service is not installed are hidden automatically.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</sect1>

</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<chapter id="manager">
<title>The Systemd Manager</title>

<para>
Start the &manager; from the <guimenu>System</guimenu> menu of the
&tde; menu or by running <command>tde-systemd-manager</command>. The window
is divided into pages that you select with the tabs at the top. Each
page loads its data the first time it is shown; press
<keycap>F5</keycap> or <guibutton>Refresh</guibutton> to load it again.
</para>

<sect1 id="manager-overview">
<title>Overview</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Overview page</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="manager-overview.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Overview page</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
The overview shows at a glance whether the system is
<emphasis>running</emphasis> normally, is <emphasis>degraded</emphasis>
(one or more units failed), is still starting up or is in maintenance
mode. Below you find the hostname, operating system, kernel, hardware,
the &systemd; version, how long the last startup took and the number of
active and failed units.
</para>
<para>
The <guilabel>Failed Units</guilabel> list contains the failed units of
the system and of your user session. <guibutton>Reset Failed
Units</guibutton> clears the failed state of all of them (the same as
<command>systemctl reset-failed</command>), <guibutton>Reload
Daemon</guibutton> makes &systemd; reread all unit files.
<guilabel>Slowest Units During Startup</guilabel> lists the units that
took longest to start (<command>systemd-analyze blame</command>).
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="manager-services">
<title>Services and Other Units</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Services page</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="manager-services.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Services page</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
The <guilabel>Services</guilabel> page lists units together with their
state. Use the controls above the list to choose what is shown:
</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Scope</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
<guilabel>System</guilabel> shows the units of the system manager
(<command>systemctl --system</command>), <guilabel>User</guilabel> the
units of your own user manager (<command>systemctl --user</command>).
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Type</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Services, sockets, timers, targets, mounts, paths,
slices or all units.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>State</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Show only active, inactive, failed, enabled, disabled,
static or masked units.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Search</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Type part of a name or description to filter the list
while you type.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>The columns of the list are:</para>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Unit</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The unit name. The icon shows whether it is active
(green check mark), failed (red cross), changing state, inactive or masked
(lock).</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Load</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Whether the unit file was loaded. <literal>not-found</literal>
units are referenced by other units but not installed; they are shown in
grey.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Active</guilabel> and <guilabel>Sub</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The high level state (active, inactive, failed, ...)
and the detailed state (running, exited, dead, ...).</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Enabled</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The unit file state: <literal>enabled</literal> units are
started automatically, <literal>disabled</literal> units are not,
<literal>static</literal> units cannot be enabled because they are only
started by other units, <literal>masked</literal> units cannot be started
at all.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Description</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>A short description from the unit file.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>
Below the list, the tabs show details of the selected unit:
<guilabel>Status</guilabel> (<command>systemctl status</command>),
<guilabel>Unit File</guilabel> including drop-in files
(<command>systemctl cat</command>), all <guilabel>Properties</guilabel>,
the <guilabel>Dependencies</guilabel> and the last 200 lines of its
<guilabel>Log</guilabel>.
</para>

<para>
The buttons at the bottom and the context menu of the list (right mouse
button) act on the selected unit:
</para>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><guibutton>Start</guibutton>, <guibutton>Stop</guibutton>,
<guibutton>Restart</guibutton>, <guibutton>Reload</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Change the runtime state of the unit. Reload asks the
service to reread its configuration without restarting.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guibutton>Enable</guibutton>, <guibutton>Disable</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Choose whether the unit is started automatically at boot
(or, for user units, when you log in).</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guibutton>Mask</guibutton>, <guibutton>Unmask</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>A masked unit cannot be started, neither manually nor as
a dependency. You are asked for confirmation before a unit is masked.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guimenuitem>Reset Failed State</guimenuitem>, <guimenuitem>Kill</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Available in the context menu.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guibutton>Reload Daemon</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Reread all unit files, needed after you edited or
installed unit files by hand.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<note><para>
Stopping essential services such as the D-Bus daemon, the login manager or
<literal>systemd-logind</literal> ends your desktop session. The
&manager; asks for confirmation before stopping these.
</para></note>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="manager-timers">
<title>Timers</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Timers page</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="manager-timers.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Timers page</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
Timers are &systemd;'s replacement for <application>cron</application>.
The <guilabel>Timers</guilabel> page lists all timers of the system or of
your user session with the unit they activate, when they run next, how
much time is left, when they ran last and how long ago that was. Select a
timer to see its status. The buttons start, stop, enable or disable the
selected timer.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="manager-datetime">
<title>Date and Time</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Date &amp; Time page</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="manager-datetime.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Date &amp; Time page</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
This page uses <command>systemd-timedated</command> and is only shown if
it is installed and enabled in the <guimenu>Settings</guimenu> menu.
</para>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Set date and time automatically</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Enables network time synchronization
(<command>systemd-timesyncd</command> or another NTP service known to
&systemd;). The status line tells you whether the clock is currently
synchronized. The option is disabled if no NTP service is installed.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Date and time</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Set the clock manually. Only possible while automatic
synchronization is switched off.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Time zone</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The system time zone.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Hardware clock runs in local time</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Only enable this if you also run an operating system that
expects the hardware clock to be in local time.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
<para>Press <guibutton>Apply</guibutton> to make the changes;
<guibutton>Reset</guibutton> reloads the current settings.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="manager-hostname">
<title>Hostname</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Hostname page</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="manager-hostname.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Hostname page</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
This page uses <command>systemd-hostnamed</command>. The <guilabel>static
hostname</guilabel> is the name of the machine on the network and may only
contain letters, digits, hyphens and dots. The <guilabel>pretty
hostname</guilabel> is a free form name shown to people, for example
<quote>Anna's Laptop</quote>. The <guilabel>icon name</guilabel>,
<guilabel>chassis</guilabel>, <guilabel>deployment</guilabel> and
<guilabel>location</guilabel> describe the machine for other programs.
<guilabel>Machine Information</guilabel> shows the machine ID, boot ID,
operating system, kernel and hardware.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="manager-locale">
<title>Locale and Keyboard</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Locale &amp; Keyboard page</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="manager-locale.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Locale &amp; Keyboard page</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
This page uses <command>systemd-localed</command> to change the
<emphasis>system wide</emphasis> locale and keyboard. They are used on the
text console, by the login manager and by programs that have no settings
of their own. Your personal &tde; language and keyboard settings from
the Control Center still take precedence inside your session.
</para>
<para>
<guilabel>Language (LANG)</guilabel> sets the main locale; the
<literal>LC_*</literal> categories can override single aspects, for example
<literal>LC_TIME</literal> for the date format or <literal>LC_PAPER</literal>
for the paper size. Only locales that are generated on the system are
offered. The keyboard section sets the console keymap and the X11 layout,
model, variant and options.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="manager-settings">
<title>Configuring the Systemd Manager</title>
<para>
The <guimenu>Settings</guimenu> menu lets you hide the optional
<guilabel>Date &amp; Time</guilabel>, <guilabel>Hostname</guilabel> and
<guilabel>Locale &amp; Keyboard</guilabel> pages, for example when the
date and time are managed by another tool. The choice is stored in
<filename>tde-systemdrc</filename> and also applies to the Control Center
module. Pages whose &systemd; service is not installed are never shown.
</para>
<para>
<menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Open Journal Viewer</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
(<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>J</keycap></keycombo>) starts the
&journalviewer;.
</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<chapter id="journal">
<title>The Journal Viewer</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Journal Viewer</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="journal-viewer.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Journal Viewer</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
Start the &journalviewer; from the <guimenu>System</guimenu> menu or by
running <command>tde-journal-viewer</command>. It shows the newest
1000 messages of the current boot; new messages are added at the bottom.
</para>

<sect1 id="journal-filters">
<title>Filtering Messages</title>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Journal</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para><guilabel>System</guilabel> shows the system journal,
<guilabel>User</guilabel> the journal of your own user session
(<command>journalctl --user</command>).</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Boot</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The current boot, an earlier boot (listed with the time
of its first and last message) or all boots. Earlier boots are only
available if the journal is stored persistently in
<filename>/var/log/journal</filename>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Priority</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Show messages of the selected priority and all more
important ones, from <guilabel>Emergency</guilabel> to <guilabel>Debug</guilabel>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Lines</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The maximum number of messages loaded.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Unit</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Only messages of one unit. The drop down list contains all
units that have messages in the journal; you can also type a name or a
pattern such as <literal>ssh*</literal>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Identifier</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Only messages with this syslog identifier, for example
<literal>sshd</literal> or <literal>sudo</literal>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Since</guilabel> and <guilabel>Until</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>A time range. You can use absolute times like
<literal>2026-11-06 10:00</literal> or expressions like
<literal>today</literal>, <literal>yesterday</literal> or
<literal>2 hours ago</literal>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Message</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Only messages whose text matches this regular expression.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Kernel messages only</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Show only messages of the kernel, like <command>dmesg</command>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Read all as administrator</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Normal users only see their own messages, unless they are
members of the <systemitem class="groupname">systemd-journal</systemitem>
(or <systemitem class="groupname">adm</systemitem>) group. With this option
the journal is read with administrator rights after authorization.
Following new messages always uses your own rights.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
<para>
Press &Enter; in a text field or <guibutton>Refresh</guibutton> to apply
the filter; <guibutton>Clear</guibutton> resets all filters. The
<guilabel>Search in results</guilabel> field filters the loaded messages
instantly without reading the journal again.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="journal-list">
<title>Reading Messages</title>
<para>
Each line shows the time, priority, source (syslog identifier or command
name), process ID and the first line of the message. Errors and more
important messages are shown in red, warnings in orange and debug
messages in grey. Select a message to see its full text and all fields
journald stored with it, such as the unit, the executable, the user ID or
the boot ID.
</para>
<para>
<guibutton>Follow</guibutton> (<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>F</keycap></keycombo>)
keeps the journal open and adds new messages as they are logged, like
<command>journalctl -f</command>. Press it again to stop following.
</para>
<para>
<menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Export...</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
(<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>S</keycap></keycombo>) saves the
messages that are currently visible to a text file, for example to attach
it to a bug report.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="journal-maintenance">
<title>Journal Maintenance</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Journal Maintenance dialog</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="journal-maintenance.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Journal Maintenance dialog</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
<menuchoice><guimenu>Tools</guimenu><guimenuitem>Journal Maintenance...</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
or the <guibutton>Maintenance...</guibutton> button open the maintenance
dialog. It shows how much disk space the journal uses and offers these
operations, each of which requires authorization:
</para>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Vacuum</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Remove old entries: reduce the archived journal files to a
maximum size, remove entries older than a number of days, weeks, months or
years, or keep only a number of archived files. Only archived files are
removed, so <guibutton>Rotate</guibutton> first if you want to include the
most recent messages. Removed messages cannot be restored.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guibutton>Rotate</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Close the active journal files, mark them as archived and
start new ones.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guibutton>Flush</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Move messages that are still kept in the volatile journal in
<filename>/run/log/journal</filename> to persistent storage in
<filename>/var/log/journal</filename>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guibutton>Sync</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Write all pending messages to disk.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guibutton>Verify</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Check all journal files for internal consistency and show
the result.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guibutton>Show Configuration</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Show the active <filename>journald.conf</filename>
including drop-in files. To limit the journal size permanently, set
<varname>SystemMaxUse=</varname> in a file such as
<filename>/etc/systemd/journald.conf.d/size.conf</filename>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
<para>The output of every operation is shown at the bottom of the dialog.</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<chapter id="kcontrol-modules">
<title>Control Center Modules</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Systemd Services module in the Control Center</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="kcontrol-systemd.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Systemd Services module</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
The &tde; Control Center contains two modules in the
<guilabel>System Administration</guilabel> category:
</para>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Systemd Services</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>All pages of the &manager;. Start it directly with
<command>tdecmshell tdesystemd</command>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>System Journal</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The &journalviewer; including the maintenance dialog.
Start it directly with <command>tdecmshell tdejournal</command>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
<para>
Changes are applied immediately by the buttons on the pages; the
<guibutton>Apply</guibutton> button of the Control Center is not used.
</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The System Journal module in the Control Center</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="kcontrol-journal.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The System Journal module</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<chapter id="authorization">
<title>Authorization and Security</title>

<para>
&tdesystemd; never runs its graphical interface with administrator
rights. When you change something on the system, it starts the small
program <command>tde-systemd-helper</command> through
<command>pkexec</command>. &polkit; then asks your authentication agent
(for example <application>polkit-agent-tde</application>) to show a
password dialog. The helper accepts only a fixed list of operations,
checks every argument (unit names, host names, time zones, sizes, ...)
and runs the matching &systemd; tool directly, without a shell.
</para>

<para>The &polkit; actions are:</para>
<informaltable>
<tgroup cols="2">
<thead><row><entry>Action</entry><entry>Used for</entry></row></thead>
<tbody>
<row><entry><literal>org.trinitydesktop.tde-systemd.units</literal></entry>
<entry>Start, stop, enable, mask, ... system units, reload the daemon</entry></row>
<row><entry><literal>org.trinitydesktop.tde-systemd.journal</literal></entry>
<entry>Journal maintenance</entry></row>
<row><entry><literal>org.trinitydesktop.tde-systemd.journal-read</literal></entry>
<entry>Reading the complete system journal</entry></row>
<row><entry><literal>org.trinitydesktop.tde-systemd.hostname</literal></entry>
<entry>Changing the hostname and machine information</entry></row>
<row><entry><literal>org.trinitydesktop.tde-systemd.timedate</literal></entry>
<entry>Changing the date, time, time zone and NTP</entry></row>
<row><entry><literal>org.trinitydesktop.tde-systemd.locale</literal></entry>
<entry>Changing the system locale and keyboard</entry></row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</informaltable>

<para>
By default an administrator password is required and remembered for a
short time. Administrators can change this with a &polkit; rule. For
example, to let members of the group <systemitem
class="groupname">sudo</systemitem> manage units without a password, create
<filename>/etc/polkit-1/rules.d/50-tde-systemd.rules</filename>:
</para>
<programlisting>polkit.addRule(function(action, subject) {
    if (action.id == "org.trinitydesktop.tde-systemd.units" &amp;&amp;
        subject.isInGroup("sudo") &amp;&amp; subject.local &amp;&amp; subject.active)
        return polkit.Result.YES;
});</programlisting>

<para>
To read the whole journal without authorization, add yourself to the
<systemitem class="groupname">systemd-journal</systemitem> group:
<userinput><command>sudo usermod -aG systemd-journal</command> <replaceable>user</replaceable></userinput>
and log in again.
</para>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<chapter id="commands">
<title>Command Line Options</title>

<para>Besides the common &tde; and Qt options, the programs accept:</para>

<sect1 id="commands-manager">
<title><command>tde-systemd-manager</command></title>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><option>--page <replaceable>name</replaceable></option></term>
<listitem><para>Show this page first: <literal>overview</literal>,
<literal>services</literal>, <literal>timers</literal>,
<literal>datetime</literal>, <literal>hostname</literal> or
<literal>locale</literal>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><option>--unit <replaceable>unit</replaceable></option></term>
<listitem><para>Show the services page and select this unit.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><option>--user</option></term>
<listitem><para>Look up <option>--unit</option> in the user manager.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="commands-journal">
<title><command>tde-journal-viewer</command></title>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><option>-u</option>, <option>--user</option></term>
<listitem><para>Show the user journal.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><option>--unit <replaceable>unit</replaceable></option></term>
<listitem><para>Show only messages of this unit.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><option>-p</option>, <option>--priority <replaceable>0-7</replaceable></option></term>
<listitem><para>Show only messages of this priority or higher.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><option>-b</option>, <option>--boot <replaceable>offset</replaceable></option></term>
<listitem><para>Show messages of this boot, for example <literal>-1</literal>
for the previous boot, or <literal>all</literal>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><option>-f</option>, <option>--follow</option></term>
<listitem><para>Follow new messages.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><option>--maintenance</option></term>
<listitem><para>Open the maintenance dialog.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
<para>
Both programs also have manual pages:
<citerefentry><refentrytitle>tde-systemd-manager</refentrytitle><manvolnum>1</manvolnum></citerefentry>,
<citerefentry><refentrytitle>tde-journal-viewer</refentrytitle><manvolnum>1</manvolnum></citerefentry> and
<citerefentry><refentrytitle>tde-systemd-helper</refentrytitle><manvolnum>8</manvolnum></citerefentry>.
</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<chapter id="faq">
<title>Questions and Answers</title>

<qandaset id="faqlist">
<qandaentry>
<question><para>Nothing happens or I get <quote>Authorization failed</quote>
when I start a service.</para></question>
<answer><para>A &polkit; authentication agent must be running in your
session. Install <application>polkit-agent-tde</application> and make sure
it is started when you log in (it is added to the &tde; autostart by its
package).</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>The journal only shows a few messages.</para></question>
<answer><para>As a normal user you only see your own messages. Enable
<guilabel>Read all as administrator</guilabel> or join the
<systemitem class="groupname">systemd-journal</systemitem> group.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>I cannot select an earlier boot.</para></question>
<answer><para>The journal is kept in memory only. Create
<filename>/var/log/journal</filename> (or set
<varname>Storage=persistent</varname> in <filename>journald.conf</filename>)
and use <guibutton>Flush</guibutton> in the maintenance dialog.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>The Date &amp; Time, Hostname or Locale page is missing.</para></question>
<answer><para>Either it was hidden in the <guimenu>Settings</guimenu> menu
or the corresponding service (<command>systemd-timedated</command>,
<command>systemd-hostnamed</command>, <command>systemd-localed</command>)
is not installed. On Debian they are part of the <filename>systemd</filename>
package; some distributions split them out.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>Can I use &tdesystemd; on a system without &systemd;?</para></question>
<answer><para>No. On systems using another init system the pages show an
error that &systemd; cannot be reached.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
</qandaset>
</chapter>

